>>
endobj
200 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Understanding data distributions is essential because they determine which statistical techniques to apply. For example, normally distributed data allows the use of parametric tests, like t-tests or ANOVA, which assume a certain distribution. Skewed or non-normal data may require transformations \(such as logarithmic or square root transformations\) to make it more suitable for analysis or necessitate the use of non-parametric methods. In a project where I analyzed income data, I encountered a highly skewed distribution, and after applying a log transformation, the data became more suitable for regression analysis.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
201 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 134 0 R
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
202 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Question 15 - How do you decide on the best way to visualize a given dataset?)
/E (Question 15 - How do you decide on the best way to visualize a given dataset?)
/Alt ()
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
203 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Appropriate Answer - )
/E (Appropriate Answer - )
/Alt ()
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
204 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (I base my choice of visualization on the data type and the story I want to tell. For trends over time, I use line charts. When comparing categories or discrete data points, bar charts are my go-to. For showing distributions, I prefer histograms or box plots. Scatter plots work well for showing relationships between two variables, and heatmaps are useful for visualizing patterns in large datasets, especially when there are multiple variables. For instance, when I worked on a project analyzing customer demographics, I used a heatmap to show the concentration of customers in different regions, which guided targeted marketing strategies.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
205 0 obj
<<
